    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d529">

                <pb facs="0618" n="544" sameAs="frus:frus1918Russiav01/pg544"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 861.00/1954</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Russia</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Francis</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Vologda</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1918-05-28T16:00:00+02:31">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">May 28, 1918, 4 p.m.</hi>
                        </date>
                        <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received June 6, 2.32 p.m.</hi>]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>215. Caldwell telegraphs Soviet government
                    granted him alone privilege of transmitting code messages and requested secrecy
                    thereof, <pb facs="0619" n="545" xml:id="pg545"/> and he so advised Department.
                    I think acceptance of special privileges unadvisable and recommend Department so
                    instruct Caldwell. Soviet government
                    industriously endeavoring to create discord between Allies especially America
                    and Japan and should not be encouraged. Please answer.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Francis</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
